<div class="section">
<h2>About</h2>
<p>
David A. Sheikh is a chemical engineer with 38 years of industrial R&amp;D experience, specializing in optical coatings for telescope mirrors. He earned his Bachelor of Science degree in Chemical Engineering from Washington University in St. Louis, Missouri, in 1987. Since then, he has dedicated his career to advancing materials engineering, particularly in the realm of optical coatings.
</p>
<p>
In 2011, David co-founded ZeCoat Corporation alongside his wife, Gillian Sheikh (business manager). Under his leadership as CEO, the company has become a leader in developing specialized vacuum coating technologies for the astronomy community. Notably, ZeCoat has engineered a unique vacuum coating system capable of uniformly coating substrates up to 2 meters in diameter with exceptional layer precision. In 2020, the company relocated from Torrance, California, to Granite City, Illinois, where it continues to innovate in optical coating solutions.
</p>
<p>
Before founding ZeCoat, David served as a Technology Director at Surface Optics Corporation (SOC) for 11 years. During his tenure, he developed the silver coating for the Kepler Space Telescope, the solar rejection filters for the NuSTAR Space Telescope, and the silver coating for the ISIS high-altitude airship.
</p>
<p>
Throughout his career, David has been instrumental in developing advanced optical coatings for both ground-based and space-based astronomy applications. His contributions include a UV-enhanced protected silver coating for NASA&rsquo;s GLIMR and OCI missions, and ongoing work on broadband coatings for far-ultraviolet (FUV) astronomy and silicon cladding for polishing lightweight mirrors. In 2014, he developed the thermal control coating applied to the seismometer cover for NASA&rsquo;s InSight mission to Mars. He also developed novel straylight absorbing coatings for space-based optical systems.
</p>
<p>
David&rsquo;s expertise encompasses large-area coatings, silicon cladding, mirror coatings, optical coating design, vacuum coatings, and thermal control coatings. His innovative work continues to advance the field of optical coatings, contributing significantly to the capabilities of modern astronomical instruments.
</p>
</div>

